+# Decision: Preview cache eviction policy (2Q)
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker keeps a private, bounded cache of rendered contact sheets (12 GiB of JPEG data, sheets near-uniform at ~2 MiB). Two workload shapes compete for it: long sequential screenings, which introduce many one-use sheets in rapid succession, and comparison sessions, in which editors repeatedly revisit a small working set of clips. A screening run that churns the cache evicts comparison sheets; a policy that clings to historically popular sheets keeps a finished production's favorites cached after editors switch projects. The external HTTP contract (200 ready / 202 pending / 404 unknown) and the render pipeline are fixed independently of this choice: every candidate policy changes only the worker's private replacement bookkeeping.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gates eliminate any option in contention — all three candidates confine their changes to the worker's private bookkeeping and respect the fixed interface, the 12 GiB byte bound, and the privacy tenet (*keep cache paths and replacement bookkeeping private to the preview worker*), which frames the boundary rather than selecting among them.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Scan resistance (major)** — while a long screening is running, previously or soon-to-be reused comparison sheets should stay cached. The selection exists primarily for this.
+- **Adaptation after a project switch (moderate)** — when editors move to a new production, the policy should let the old working set go and warm the new one at normal pace.
+- **Bookkeeping cost (moderate)** — per-hit and per-admission memory and CPU work on a single eight-core worker serving 24 editors. All candidates are cheap in absolute terms; the criterion separates *how* the cost is paid (state size, per-hit writes, structure count).
+- **Tuning burden (moderate)** — parameters the team must set before the policy behaves well, and the damage a mis-set parameter can do during the pilot.
+- **Re-render cost on churned entries (moderate)** — a miss costs a ~450 ms render plus queue wait; a policy that wrongly demotes a comparison sheet pays this on the next visit.
+
+Latent criteria probed and dropped: per-editor fairness (no candidate addresses it, and the pilot's single worker does not either); per-entry memory overhead (trivial for all candidates at ~6,000 entries against a 12 GiB byte budget); operational predictability (subsumed by bookkeeping cost and tuning burden).
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU (single recency queue)
+
+One queue ordered by last reference; a hit refreshes recency, an admission evicts the least-recently-used entry.
+
+- Scan resistance: **weak** — a sequential screening touches every sheet once, which is enough under LRU to promote each scan entry ahead of the comparison working set, evicting it mid-screening.
+- Adaptation after a switch: **strong** — nothing outlives its recency; a new production's working set warms immediately.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **strong** — one queue, one move per hit; the cheapest of the three.
+- Tuning burden: **strong** — no parameters beyond the total byte bound.
+- Re-render cost on churned entries: **weak** — churn is precisely what LRU does to the comparison set during screenings.
+
+Summary: the cheapest policy, at the price of losing the comparison working set exactly when screenings run.
+
+### Frequency counting with aging
+
+Per-key hit counters decayed by an aging factor; eviction removes the lowest aged count.
+
+- Scan resistance: **partial** — a one-use scan entry has count 1 and loses to comparison sheets with accumulated counts, so the working set survives; but a *large* comparison set built during quiet periods can itself be demoted slowly by aging while a screening runs.
+- Adaptation after a switch: **weak** — aged counts keep yesterday's favorites cached well after editors have moved on, lagging until decay catches up.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **weak** — every hit rewrites a counter and every aging tick touches every key.
+- Tuning burden: **weak** — two coupled parameters (counter granularity, aging rate) with a bad aging rate producing either LRU behavior or permanent favorites.
+- Re-render cost on churned entries: **partial** — comparison sheets are usually retained, but entries aged out between sessions pay a re-render.
+
+Summary: keeps popular sheets but pays continuous counter maintenance and adapts slowly when popularity shifts.
+
+### 2Q (first-use and reused queues)
+
+Two queues: completed sheets enter a FIFO first-use queue on first render; a second served reference promotes the sheet to an LRU reused queue. Each queue holds a share of the byte budget; screening pressure fills and evicts within the first-use queue and cannot displace a reused sheet.
+
+- Scan resistance: **strong** — one-use screening sheets live and die in the first-use queue; a comparison sheet is promoted by its second reference and is thereafter displaced only by other promoted sheets, never by screening traffic.
+- Adaptation after a switch: **strong** — the reused queue's LRU order and the first-use queue's FIFO both turn over with the current workload; nothing accumulates authority the way aged counts do.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **partial** — a second queue and a promotion move on qualifying hits; still O(1) per event and no per-hit counter writes.
+- Tuning burden: **partial** — one parameter (the byte split between queues) whose mis-setting degrades toward LRU-at-reduced-capacity rather than toward a pathological state.
+- Re-render cost on churned entries: **partial** — a comparison sheet falls out of the first-use queue before earning a second reference pays a re-render; the ghost-key variant (recording evicted first-use keys so a prompt re-reference admits the re-render directly to the reused queue) trades more bookkeeping for less of this cost, and remains an open question in the design doc.
+
+Summary: structural scan resistance — reused sheets are protected by position, not by counted popularity.
+
+## Selection
+
+**2Q.** The decision turns on scan resistance, the major criterion, where 2Q is strong and LRU — the otherwise cheapest and simplest candidate — is weakest; the workload the worker exists for alternates long screenings with repeated comparison sessions, so the comparison working set must survive a screening to make the cache worth its 12 GiB. Against frequency-with-aging, 2Q wins on adaptation after a project switch and on bookkeeping cost: it protects reused sheets by their position in a second queue rather than by counters that must be maintained on every hit and aged against last week's favorites. 2Q's costs are accepted deliberately: a second queue and a byte split that must be tuned on the observed screening/comparison mix during the pilot (tracked as an open question in the design doc).
+
+No tenet drives the choice among these candidates; the privacy tenet frames it — every option keeps all changes inside the preview worker's private replacement bookkeeping, and 2Q adds no externally visible surface: the 200/202/404 contract, the render queue, and the 12 GiB bound are unchanged. Committing to 2Q forecloses single-queue simplicity and obligates the split tuning and the promotion/eviction rules carried in the design doc; it does not foreclose revisiting the policy later, since the interface the review API consumes is policy-independent by design.
